  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "claim_63875e14be604960b86d1c0b7cbbf9bc",
      "statement": "internal/wrapper uses github.com/creack/pty on non-Windows (pty_unix.go's ptyProcess) and github.com/aymanbagabas/go-pty's ConPTY-backed conPtyProcess on Windows (pty_windows.go), selected via build tags, because Windows has no native POSIX PTY and ConPTY is the OS's own equivalent pseudoconsole primitive.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://internal/wrapper/pty_unix.go#L1-L15",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:5fe790d9b3ca2189fa8722face8914a1a2ec87c55583b401c5a4f82273959d3d:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://internal/wrapper/pty_windows.go#L1-L15",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:5f2f5607804aa2c12ad442472274e0ea4028b0402bc77153345c7cdf471444a1:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_9201eda6bde64bac84b0fbf8b8147038",
      "statement": "ptyProcess.Close on Unix is idempotent and layered: the first call asks the child to exit and closes the PTY master, escalating to SIGKILL only if Wait does not complete within a grace period, and subsequent calls are no-ops.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://internal/wrapper/pty_unix.go#L28-L31",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:e80b00e089d58c8cf29a40c06b95d5a907e6ae46fe464b33db85786e433b4334:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_65a9dd14dc124abba3468fb25568f3f9",
      "statement": "wrapperSession.maxProcessedInputSeq tracks the highest pty_input sequence number the wrapper has actually finished writing to the PTY, because the Hub resends an unacknowledged input frame using its original sequence number and a WebSocket disconnect between a PTY write completing and its ack reaching the Hub can lose that ack (abortCurrentConn closes without waiting on the send mutex); without this guard a resend would write the same bytes, including a trailing carriage return, to the PTY a second time, silently submitting an extra Enter against whatever prompt is showing next.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://internal/wrapper/wrapper.go#L84-L98",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:c2b4d18b2a683b387ac6311887daeb6906bb96d3eefede4a0e268ec980fcd280:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_02e2af222e8a4693b23246da68bf8d42",
      "statement": "inputSeqAlreadyProcessed treats any sequence number at or below the recorded maximum as an already-applied resend and acknowledges it without re-writing to the PTY; markInputSeqProcessed is only called after a PTY write actually succeeds so a failed write's sequence number is never marked done, with the accepted residual gap being that a later successful write past a failed one causes the failed write's resend to also be treated as already-processed, judged acceptable because a PTY write only fails when the PTY itself is broken, which already ends the session.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://internal/wrapper/wrapper.go#L94-L106",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:2ad9d9769a54af327b49e6b8efd6d9db308aaaeb11a5b3a13f80e7561e4d5e9f:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_ab3adf1db0304698bd60f3348db190b3",
      "statement": "The reconnectSupervisor treats a WebSocket disconnect as a genuinely intentional disconnect, closing the PTY immediately, only when all four hold at once: the disconnect was not flagged intentional-only-by-name (i.e., it passes the intentional check), no local transport fault occurred, the wrapper did not just reattach within postReattachGuard, and a fresh probe confirms the Hub is still alive.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://internal/wrapper/wrapper.go#L296-L315",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:5a85ee64e73626df7dacb31e9f9de5c8eccfc4653fb160facfa0af3a7e72b335:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_94dcc2d4d3404d4b94b26e62c156d650",
      "statement": "A local PTY-output transport fault (the wrapper's own send queue filling up or a write deadline being hit) is explicitly not treated as proof the Hub went down, because without that distinction a transient full output queue or slow write would kill the wrapped CLI and all of its own child agents while the Hub was still healthy, even with auto_shutdown enabled.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://internal/wrapper/wrapper.go#L316-L333",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:054942dff338199571556beb9e13c1c87998fbbc8ddcc4ef9dd956edb3d13960:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_1ba43de8b4ca4c528f2feb20372f42a0",
      "statement": "A disconnect within postReattachGuard (10 seconds) of a successful reattach is given the same reconnect-grace benefit of the doubt as a transport fault, even though a bare post-reattach WebSocket EOF never actually goes through the transport-fault code path, added specifically after a bug where a Codex reattach's plain EOF was misclassified as an intentional kill even after the queue-full transport-fault case was already fixed.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://internal/wrapper/wrapper.go#L321-L327",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:71abc4cac705f9329d1a7e162847df16ab3983f78c78c8d5d9dfcfa39f84521b:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://internal/wrapper/wrapper.go#L49-L55",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:ffa627144ed50aedddb9cbe4405ffd0b2d9525db4816caab41d4ab4d1b9a7eda:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_5f45e64c8e534b969c573716ab156765",
      "statement": "reconnectGrace reads hub.wrapper_reconnect_grace_sec from config and returns zero duration (disabling reconnect entirely, restoring immediate-kill behavior) when the configured value is negative or zero.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://internal/wrapper/wrapper.go#L58-L66",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:5adb92fc3f0778d31b4eb4f65cb0d4c15746ae69153c030a3d2170681f243e9a:"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
